<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.5.4: Using Function Notation and Graphing Technology</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00925</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.5.4: Using Function Notation and Graphing Technology</md:title>
    <md:uuid>eb44e091-9498-4b0e-b2b5-85f7f3be5b7f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>The function \(B\) is defined by the equation \(B(x) = 10x + 25\). Use the graphing tool here or technology outside the course. Graph the function.</para>
<iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><para>If needed, remember -</para>
<list>
  <item>You can collapse the equation entry tab by selecting the button.</item>
  <item>To change the scale along either the x- or \(y\)-axis, click on the wrench button and then enter minimum and maximum values for each axis and set the step value.</item>
  <item>To find a point on the line, click on the line and drag the mouse along the line to display the ordered pairs for each location.</item>
</list>

<!--Question 1 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the value of \(B(6)\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(B(6) = 85\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 2 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What were the window values you used for the \(x\)-axis? (Write the values as an inequality.)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(-1 \leq x \leq 7\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 3 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What were the window values you used for the \(y\)-axis? (Write the values as an inequality.)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(-1 \leq y \leq 100\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 4 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the value of \(B(2.75)\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(B(2.75) = 52.5\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 5 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the value of \(B(1.482)\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(B(1.482) = 39.8\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 6 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the value of \(x\) if \(B(x) = 93\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x = 6.8\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 7 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What were the window values you used for the \(x\)-axis? (Write the values as an inequality.)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(-2 \leq x \leq 16\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 8 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What were the window values you used for the \(y\)-axis? (Write the values as an inequality.)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(-15 \leq y \leq 150\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 9 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the value of \(x\) if \(B(x) = 42.1\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x = 1.71\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>

<!--Question 10 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What is the value of \(x\) if \(B(x) = 116.25\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x = 9.125\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise></section>

  <note class="self-check"><para>The function \(D\) is defined by the equation \(D(x)=2.5x +12\).</para>
    <para>Which describes the window that can be used to find the value of \(x\) when \(D(x)=23.25\) using graphing technology and gives the correct value of \(x\)?</para></note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section>
      <title>Finding Missing Values</title>
      <para>Function \(G\) is defined by \(G(x)=5x+20\). Find the following values using graphing technology.</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>\(G(2)\)</item>
      </list>
      <para>To find \(G(2)\), look at \(x=2\) on the \(x\)-axis on the graph of \(G(x)\). Use proper steps with your graphing technology to find the \(y\)-value on the line: \(G(2)=30\).<figure><media alt="GRAPH OF A STRAIGHT LINE WITH \(y\)-intercepts OF 20 THAT PASSES THROUGH THE POINT (2, 30)."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_11_01.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated">
        <item>\(G(x)=32\)</item>
      </list>
      <para>To find the value of \(x\) where \(G(x)=32\), look at where the graph crosses the \(y\)-value of 32. Use proper steps with your graphing technology to find the \(x\)-value where the graph meets \(y=32\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF A STRAIGHT LINE WITH \(y\)-intercepts OF 20 THAT PASSES THROUGH THE POINT (2.4, 32). A GREEN HORIZONTAL LINE THAT PASSES THROUGH (0, 32).">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_11_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>\(G(2.4)=32\)</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Finding Missing Values</title>
      <para>Function \(H\) is defined by \(H(x)=2x+4\). Find \(H(x)=10.4\) using graphing technology.</para>
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/a47b5bb4ba783be55a7067b919f165f14dcc36ce.html" width="500" height="400"/><example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Use the graphing tool here or technology outside the course. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to find the value of \(H(x)\) using graphing technology:To find the value of \(x\) where \(H(x)=10.4\), look at where the graph of \(H(x)=2x+4\) crosses the \(y\)-value of 10.4. Use proper steps with your graphing technology to find the \(x\)-value where the graph meets \(y=10.4\).</para>
            <para>\(H(3.2)=10.4\)</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="GRAPH OF A STRAIGHT LINE WITH \(y\)-intercepts OF 4 THAT PASSES THROUGH THE POINT (3.2, 10.4). A RED HORIZONTAL LINE THAT PASSES THROUGH (0, 10.4)">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_08_11_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
    </section>
    </section>
    
